i won't be offended but let's say  that we have a set of training data that  includes the value that you're trying to  predict like we do with our iris data  so we don't have to guess if our  resulting model is good or not you know  we can actually take those known answers  if you will and compare how well our  model does at predicting them and the  trick that we do here is by splitting it  up we have a training data set and a  testing data set the idea is to only  train the model on the training data and  we hold out that test data to see how  well that model does on predicting  classifications or values whatever it is  on data that it's never seen before if  you were training your model on all of  your data that's cheating right like  you've basically given your model the  answers ahead of time so it wouldn't  really make sense to train your model on  a data set that includes your test data  you want to reserve that off to the side  and use that just to evaluate your model  and then you can measure the accuracy of  how well your model does that predicting  the known correct answers in your test  set uh there are various metrics for  doing that like r squared and we just  compare it you know we just look at how  good it is at that point and we use that  to figure out is my model getting better  or worse as i sort of tuned the  parameters on it  now in practice uh we end up with this  uh thing called overfitting and that we  can run into so  the danger here in train test is that  you can end up sort of overfitting to  these sort of random variations in the  data that don't really have anything to  do with the intrinsic values of the data  itself so imagine that we're trying to  predict whether a dot should be red or  blue  based on you know some attributes  associated with each dot you know maybe  each dot represents the height and  weight of a person or something i don't  know  but the  the risk here is that you might end up  with a model that does this like crazy  curvy line to try to like get every  answer right so the accuracy of this  crazy curvy model would be 100 because  it's really going out of its way to make  sure that you know every answer that you  gave it in the training data is going to  be the right one this is called  overfitting right so obviously this  isn't representing anything real this  would not generalize well to other data  because our model is basically  overfitting to the training data that we  gave it a better model would be a  simpler one that just has sort of this  black curve here that's probably more of  a representation of what's going on in  reality as a term in terms of what  attributes result in what classification  so train test kind of addresses that  because by testing this model against  data that's never seen before  an overfitted model would not do well on  that it doesn't generalize like we said  to data that's never seen before so by  reserving that test data we make sure  that we're measuring how well this  this model does on data that's never  seen before and so that will penalize it  for overfitting  however train test is not perfect either  you know if you have a small data set  which we do in the case of the iris data  uh there is a chance that your training  data set in your test data set might be  weird in the same way right so take a  look at this example here on the right  our training data set and our test data  set actually have fairly similar  patterns here that it can overfit to so  an overfitted model based on this  training data would actually perform  pretty well on this test data because  just by chance  our test data has some of the same  random fluctuations that our training  data did as well so overfitting can  still happen when you're doing train  tests  so how do we get around that with  something called k-fold cross-validation  so a way to sort of further predict  protect against overfitting is by doing  this multiple times basically so instead  of just having a single training set in  a single test set what we do is this we  take our data and we split it up into k  different randomly assigned segments  and we go through each one of those  segments and in each step we take one of  those segments and reserve that as our  test data and take the remaining  segments and use that as our training  data and then we go through each segment  and do it again so basically we're doing  train test k times where we're taking a  different segment of that data that's  been assigned at random as our test data  and the remaining is the training data  so this if you had you know k-fold  cross-validation with 10 folds you could  do train tests 10 different times and  then basically average the results of  how  well the accuracy was on each iteration  to get a more robust measure of just how  good your accuracy is so that's what we  mean by k-fold cross-validation and in  the context of hxg boost that's how it's  evaluating at each step of its training  as to whether or not you're getting a  good result so it's doing  cross-validation and saying okay in the  most robust manner that i know of how  good is my model doing on the data  you've given it and if it's good enough  at that point it can say okay i'm done i  can i can early stop  let's get back to xg boost with all that  uh theory behind us  so like we mentioned if you want to  install it within a python environment  it's super simple you just have to say  pip install xgboost and you're done but  xg boost is not just for python it's  actually a big standalone open source  project of its own and it has a command  line interface of its own it has a c  plus interface r julia interfaces for  java virtual machines so it's not just  made for scikit-learn you can actually  use xgboost within pretty much any  application you want to which is pretty  cool  and because it is more general it's not  just for scikit-learn it doesn't have a  scikit-learn specific interface so  because of that that's 