y different combinations across wide  ranges of values for each parameter and  as you can imagine that is an extremely  intensive in terms of computing  requirements i mean that means you have  to train your model and test it and  evaluate it many many many many times so  if you have this matrix of many  different parameters and you're trying  different values on each one of those  parameters that explodes really quickly  that's a big part of why training  machine learning model is as expensive  as it is and uses as much energy as it  does because you're trying to converge  on those hyper parameters there are  technologies out there that make it  easier and simpler and faster for  example amazon web services has a  hyperparameter tuning job as part of its  sagemaker service that tries to do this  a little bit more intelligently you know  it tries to figure out as you're going  through the tuning process  what parameters are having the biggest  effect and sort of converges on them  over time to you know make that a little  bit more quick and it has the obviously  the  the expansive um hardware resources  available to you on aws but again it's  not cheap  so lots of knobs and dials with xg boost  that you can play with and unfortunately  getting them right is often just a  matter of trial and error  but yeah xg boost is kind of awesome you  know it's almost all that you need to  know for machine learning in practical  terms i mean for any classification  problem or even regression problems  there's a it can do that as well uh  regression is where we're trying to  predict a numeric value as opposed to  predicting a classification like what  species of uh a flower do i have so  let's go back to the code again and go  into a little bit more depth this time  and see it in action  all right so we're back at my notebook  here  let's uh go through this one more time  and then we'll call it good so again  we're just using xg boost here and  showing you how easy it is  let's r